            <ArticleTitle>Mucinous adenocarcinoma of trachea: a rare case of primary tracheal tumor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>363-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0218492312454857</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A primary tracheal tumor is not common, but adenocarcinoma is particularly rare. We describe a case of primary tracheal mucinous adenocarcinoma misdiagnosed as asthma in a 17-year-old girl. Computed tomography revealed an intraluminal partially obstructing mass in the mid portion of the trachea. A sleeve resection of the involved trachea with primary anastomosis was performed, followed by adjuvant radiotherapy. After 23 months of follow-up, there has been no recurrence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
